Two of them can be absent rather than zero. A transient needs a note starting
and a decay needs one ending, so a reading holding neither has no answer instead
of an answer of nothing. Three more can be absent because they were added after
the first sweeps ran.

## Adding to them

```bash
# every block, one reading each. --resume skips what is already there.
tonestack measure blocks --resume --out resources/sweeps/hx-stomp/fingerprints.json

# one block, every control, each measured alone
tonestack measure controls --model {{ .Model }} \
    --out resources/sweeps/hx-stomp/{{ .Model }}.json

# check the catalog's parameter order against the hardware
tonestack measure names --model {{ .Model }}
```

Each of these puts the block in a chain of its own and plays it through
`presets play` rather than writing a slot, because a slot is flash and a
campaign loads a chain once per control. The chain is played again before every
control: a live edit writes nothing back, so without that each sweep would run
on a chain the previous one left skewed.

A control's range comes from the catalog. That is not a nicety, because many of
the device's float controls do not run zero to one, and a Simple EQ's Mid Freq
swept 0..1 never leaves its bottom stop and reports as a control that does
nothing.

`just pack-measured` puts the fingerprints into the binary, through the same
reader the binary uses, so a library that will not load is refused there rather
than at the next build. Only the fingerprints are packed; the curves stay in
the tree.
